    "final_answer": "I now have full data for both class years. Let me provide a comprehensive answer based on the NCAAW Recruiting rankings on ESPN.\n\n## Summary of NCAAW Recruiting (espnW Rankings / SportsCenter NEXT 100 Girls)\n\nThe NCAAW (NCAA Women's) recruiting page on ESPN shows the **espnW Rankings** (also called the **SportsCenter NEXT 100 Girls' Basketball**). The default class year is **2027**, where most top players are still uncommitted. Looking at the previous class (**2026**), all three top players are committed, providing a clearer answer to the question.\n\n### Top 3 Players and Their Colleges:\n\n**Class of 2026 (all top 3 are committed):**\n| Rank | Player | Position | College |\n|------|--------|----------|---------|\n| 1 | Saniyah Hall | G | **USC** |\n| 2 | Olivia Vukosa | P | **UConn** |\n| 3 | Oliviyah Edwards | F | **South Carolina** |\n\nThe top three NCAAW recruits are committed to **USC, UConn, and South Carolina**.\n\n### Class of 2027 (default view, most top players still uncommitted):\n| Rank | Player | Position | College |\n|------|--------|----------|---------|\n| 1 | Kaleena Smith | PG | Uncommitted |\n| 2 | Ivanna Wilson Manyacka | W | Uncommitted |\n| 3 | Caroline Bradley | P | **LSU** |\n\nFor the most recent class (2027), only Caroline Bradley has committed — to **LSU**. The other two top recruits (Kaleena Smith and Ivanna Wilson Manyacka) are still uncommitted.\n\n### Answer:\n- **Class of 2026 (the most meaningful answer):** The top 3 recruits are from **USC, UConn, and South Carolina**.\n- **Class of 2027 (default view):** Only **LSU** is represented in the top 3 (for #3 Caroline Bradley); the top two players are still uncommitted.",
